Q1: Rate limiter check ka acceptable latency overhead kya hai — normal aur peak load pe p99 ? Koi SLA defined hai? 
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Rate limiting mai latency aane ke reasons kya hai ?
------------------------------------------------------

Source 1: Rule Fetching Latency
-----------------------------------

Agar dynamic config hai, to rules 3-layer architecture (Local Cache → Redis → DB) se fetch honge:

Layer	Latency	                    Kab Hit Hoga
Local Cache	~0ms (ammortized)     99% redis rule fetch requests local cache se hi serve ho jaati hai.
Redis	1-3ms	                      Local cache ka TTL expire hone pe (cold path)
DB 10-50ms	                      Redis miss pe (rare)

Key insight: Ye latency har request pe nahi lagti. Sirf TTL expire hone pe lagti hai so average latency pe negligible impact.


Source 2: Counter Operation Latency (Redis Call)
--------------------------------------------------

Har request pe Redis call karni padegi — GET / INCR — counter check aur update ke liye.
Ye latency (1-3ms) unavoidable hai distributed setup me, kyunki Multiple application instances hote hain
and sabko same shared counter chahiye. Agar har instance apna local counter rakhe, to rate limit
approx. ho jayegi (rate limit inconsistency)

### NOTE:
----------
Agar < 2ms latency chahiye, to local counter (per-instance)  use karna padega. Isse latency ~0ms ho jayegi,
but rate limiting approximate ho jayegi (under-allow). Matlab consistency lose ho jayegi.


Source 3: Connection Pool Wait Time
-------------------------------------

- Maan lo 100 application threads hain, lekin Redis connection pool sirf 50 connections ka hai. Agar 80 requests aa rahi hain, to 50 threads ko turant 
  connection mil jaayega (kyunki pool me 50 connections available hain), lekin remaining 30 threads wait karenge connection available hone ka. Ye wait 
  time latency me add hota hai — peak load pe 5–20ms tak ja sakta hai.

- Solution: Connection pool size tune karna (Lettuce/Jedis config ke through).

- Lekin pool size client pe depend karta hai:

- Jedis blocking hai — 1 Redis connection = 1 Tomcat thread at a time. Peak pe jitni concurrent Redis calls hongi, utne hi Tomcat threads unhe execute karenge, 
  aur utne hi Redis connections chahiye — worst case me ye concurrent requests ke barabar hote hain (jab saari requests Redis call karti hain).

- Lettuce (Spring Boot ka default Redis client with `spring-boot-starter-data-redis`) multiplexing support karta hai, jisme multiple Tomcat threads ek hi Redis
  connection share karke multiple commands execute kar sakte hain. Isliye generally bada connection pool maintain karne ki zaroorat nahi hoti; relatively chhota
  pool sufficient ho sakta hai.



Source 4: Serialization/Deserialization
-----------------------------------------
Serialization/Deserialization latency sirf cold path pe hoti hai — jab rule DB se fetch (JSON → Java object) ya insert/update (Java object → JSON) ho. 
Har request pe nahi. Mostly requests Local Cache se serve ho jaati hain, kuch Redis tak jaati hain, aur rarely DB tak pahunchti hain. Isliye ye latency
rare hai. Isliye ~0.1–0.5ms latency average pe negligible hai.



Source 5: Rate limiter fail hone par bhi latency introduce ho sakti hai 
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

- Agar FR me fail-closed choose kiya — Redis down pe requests reject. Fail-open choose kiya — requests allow.
- Circuit Breaker ke bina: Har request Redis connect try karegi aur connection timeout tak wait karegi (retry configured ho to har attempt pe wait).
  Uske baad decision hoga (reject ya allow, mode ke hisaab se). Isse latency add ho jaati hai.
- Circuit Breaker ke saath: Redis failures track hote hain. Threshold cross hone par circuit OPEN — ab requests bina Redis call try kiye immediately decide
  ho jaati hain:

    a) Fail-closed → fallback error (503/429) → ~0ms
    b) Fail-open → directly allow → ~0ms

- Initial window me latency abhi bhi hogi: Circuit ko OPEN hone ke liye kuch sample failures chahiye. Jab tak threshold cross nahi hota, tab tak Redis
  connect attempts aur timeout waits hote rehte hain, latency rahegi. Sirf threshold cross hone ke baad latency ~0ms hoti hai.



CASE 1: Interviewer bole "p99 < 5ms (strict) / No strict SLA / tum decide karo
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Redis round-trip counter check mai 1-3ms chala jaata hai, avoid nahi ho sakta (distributed counter). Remaining ~2ms buffer me ye 3 cheezein tune karo::

1. Dynamic rules ke liye 3-layer architecture + TTL tuning - 
   - Agar FR mai dynamic rule config. decide kiya hai to  Local Cache → Redis → DB layered architecture use karo. Local Cache TTL aur Redis TTL ko 
     carefully tune karo — taaki hot path pe 99% requests Local Cache se serve hon (microseconds), aur Redis/DB sirf cold path pe jaayein. Isse rule
     fetching latency average pe negligible ho jaati hai.
 

2. Circuit Breaker ko jaldi OPEN hone ke liye tune karo
    - minimumNumberOfCalls aur failureRateThreshold thoda kam rakho — taaki Redis down hone par circuit jaldi OPEN ho

       a) minimumNumberOfCalls  — CB tab tak failure rate calculate nahi karta jab tak itni calls na ho jaayein. Isse false positive avoid hota hai 
          warna 2 calls me 1 fail pe 50% rate ban jaayega (agar failureRateThreshold = 50%) aur ek minor blip pe CB OPEN ho jaayega.

       b) failureRateThreshold — minimumNumberOfCalls complete hone ke baad hi actual failure rate count shuru hoti hai. Uske baad agar actual failure 
          rate is % se zyada ho → circuit OPEN. Example: minimumNumberOfCalls = 20, failureRateThreshold = 50% — last 20 calls me 10+ fail → 50%+ → OPEN. 

3. Redis connection pool tune karo
  - Pool size tune karo taaki peak load pe threads ko connection ke liye wait na karna pade. 
  - Pool exhaust hone par threads 5–20ms wait karte hain — jo p99 5ms budget ko blow kar dega. 
  - Client ke hisaab se tune karo: Lettuce (multiplexing) me chhota pool, Jedis me concurrent Redis calls ke close.




CASE 2: Interviewer bole "p99 < 3ms 
--------------------------------------

Redis counter round-trip ka p99 itself 1–3ms hota hai. Distributed counter < 3ms me fit nahi hoga — ye fundamental constraint hai, tuning se solve nahi hota.

2 options hain:

1: Local counter per instance (approximate rate limiting)
   Har instance apni local memory me counter rakhega — per-instance limit = total allowed limit / number of instances (jaise 300 req/window/user, 3 instances → 100 per instance).
   
    Problem — uneven routing: LB ne Instance-1 pe 150 requests route kar di. Instance-1 ne pehli 100 allow ki, remaining 50 reject — kyunki us instance ka local 
    counter exhaust ho gaya. Lekin global counter abhi reach nahi hua — 300 me se sirf 100 use hue, bache 200 count Instance-2 aur Instance-3 ke paas available
    hain, par Instance-1 unhe access nahi kar sakta (counter local hai, shared nahi). Result: 50 legitimate requests unnecessarily reject — sirf isliye ki load 
    ek instance pe zyada tha.

    Auto-scaling problem: Per-instance limit = total / N — N change hote hi (scale up/down) recalculate karna padega. Mid-flight windows reset ho jaati hain.


2: Rule fetching ke liye:
   Static prefer (no runtime fetch). Dynamic chahiye to 3-layer (Local Cache → Redis → DB) + TTL lambi (rules rarely change → Redis/DB hits minimum).



